<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical="https://divorceu.ae/en/divorce-timeline-uae/"
  lang="en"
  hreflang={hreflang}
  schema={articleSchema}
  lastmod="2026-11-13"
>
  <Breadcrumb items={breadcrumbs} />

  <section class="hero-section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="overline">UAE Divorce Guide · Updated 2026</p>
      <h1>How Long Does Divorce Take in UAE? Complete Timeline</h1>
      <p class="byline">By Aleksi Suon, UAE Expat &amp; Legal Consultant · Updated November 2026</p>
      <p class="hero-sub">From Family Guidance to the final judgment, here is what the law actually fixes at each stage of a UAE divorce under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024 and the civil law for non-Muslims, what the rest depends on, and what speeds a case up or slows it down.</p>
      <div class="hero-cta" style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:12px;margin-top:24px;">
        <a href="#contact" style="display:inline-block;padding:13px 28px;background:#c9a84c;color:#fff;border-radius:8px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:700;">Request a Free Consultation</a>
        <a href="https://wa.me/971557344876?text=Hello%2C%20I%20need%20divorce%20legal%20advice%20in%20UAE" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:8px;padding:13px 28px;background:#25d366;color:#fff;border-radius:8px;text-decoration:none;font-weight:700;">Chat on WhatsApp</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <main class="main-content">
    <figure style="margin:0 0 8px;">
      <img src="/images/divorce-timeline-uae.webp" alt="UAE divorce timeline: a calendar and hourglass beside legal documents on a desk" width="1200" height="675" loading="eager" decoding="async" style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px;" />
    </figure>
    <div class="container">

      <div class="highlight-box">
        <h2>UAE Divorce Timeline: Quick Reference</h2>
        <div class="timeline-cards">
          {timelineCards.map(c => (
            <div class="tl-card">
              <div class="tl-type">{c.type}</div>
              <div class="tl-duration">{c.headline}</div>
              <div class="tl-meta">
                <span>{c.law}</span>
              </div>
              <div class="tl-note">{c.note}</div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="table-caveat" style="margin:1rem 0 0;">No law sets an overall duration for a divorce. Each card shows only what the law fixes for that route; the rest depends on agreement, service on the other spouse, expert reports and appeals.</p>
      </div>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Stage-by-Stage: What Happens at Each Step</h2>
        <p>Most Muslim divorces in the UAE follow the same sequence, from Family Guidance (if the judge refers the case) through to the final judgment and the iddah. Non-Muslims filing under the civil law are not referred to Family Guidance. The law fixes only a few of these time limits; where a stage has none, we say what it depends on. What varies most is the evidence and hearings stage of a contested case. For the underlying rules see our overview of <a href="/en/uae-divorce-law/">UAE divorce law</a> and the split between <a href="/en/contested-vs-uncontested-divorce-uae/">contested and uncontested divorce</a>.</p>
        <div class="stages-list">
          {stages.map(s => (
            <div class="stage-card">
              <div class="stage-header">
                <div class="stage-num">{s.num}</div>
                <div class="stage-head-text">
                  <h3>{s.title}</h3>
                  <span class="stage-duration">{s.duration}</span>
                </div>
              </div>
              <p>{s.desc}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Timeline by Route: A Comparison</h2>
        <p>There is no single UAE divorce timeline. The route you qualify for, and whether you both agree, decides which time limits apply to you. The comparison table below maps each route to its governing law, whether Family Guidance applies, and the time limits the law fixes. Non-Muslim expats should read our comparison of <a href="/en/dubai-vs-abu-dhabi-divorce/">Dubai versus Abu Dhabi divorce</a> before choosing where to file, and our guide to <a href="/en/mutual-consent-divorce-uae/">mutual consent divorce</a> if you both agree.</p>
        <div class="comparison-table">
          <div class="comparison-header">
            <span>Route</span>
            <span>Governing Law</span>
            <span>Family Guidance</span>
            <span>Fixed by Law</span>
            <span>Notes</span>
          </div>
          {routeCompare.map(r => (
            <div class="comparison-row">
              <span class="comp-route">{r.route}</span>
              <span>{r.law}</span>
              <span>{r.recon}</span>
              <span class="comp-duration">{r.fixed}</span>
              <span class="comp-notes">{r.notes}</span>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
        <p class="table-caveat">The table shows only the time limits written into the law. Everything else depends on the court’s schedule, service on the other spouse, expert reports and appeals. For the Abu Dhabi civil procedure step by step, read our guide to <a href="/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-abu-dhabi/">how to file for divorce in Abu Dhabi</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>The Family Guidance Stage: Only If the Judge Refers You</h2>
        <p>For Muslim divorces, Family Guidance is a possible first stage, not an automatic one. Under Article 8 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, in force since 15 April 2025, the supervising judge may refer the spouses to Family Guidance before the case goes to court, if the judge considers an amicable settlement feasible. Wills, inheritance, urgent and temporary cases (including urgent orders on alimony and custody) and cases where reconciliation is not foreseeable, such as cases to prove a marriage or a divorce, are exempt. The law sets no time limit for this stage, so how long it takes depends on the court and on the spouses.</p>
        <p>A counsellor meets both spouses and tries to settle the dispute. A settlement is recorded in a report signed by the spouses and the counsellor and approved by the supervising judge; it has the force of an execution writ and cannot be appealed unless it contradicts the law. In Dubai, the Dubai Courts Family Conciliation service has no fee and ends in a settlement, a waiver, file closure or a referral to court, and a personal status claim is filed with that referral attached. Non-Muslims divorcing under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 are not referred to Family Guidance (Article 3). Our dedicated guide to the <a href="/en/family-guidance-section-uae/">Family Guidance Section</a> explains what happens in each session.</p>
        <p>The 60-day figure you may have read about belongs to a different stage. In contested divorce claims based on harm or discord, the court appoints two arbitrators to try to reconcile the spouses and report back. Article 72 of the 2024 law gives them up to 60 days from their appointment; the old law allowed up to 90. That limit shapes contested harm cases, not an agreed divorce.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>What Causes Delays, and How to Avoid Them</h2>
        <p>Many delays can be avoided with preparation. The table below covers the common ones, what the law says about each, and what to do. Serving a spouse who has left the UAE is one of the biggest, so flag an absent spouse to your lawyer at the first meeting. A post-judgment appeal is the other; our <a href="/en/divorce-appeal-uae/">divorce appeal guide</a> covers the 30-day window and how to decide whether to appeal, and delays also feed into the total <a href="/en/divorce-cost-uae/">divorce cost in UAE</a>.</p>
        <div class="delay-table">
          <div class="delay-header">
            <span>Delay Factor</span>
            <span>Effect on Time</span>
            <span>How to Avoid</span>
          </div>
          {delayFactors.map(d => (
            <div class="delay-row">
              <span class="delay-factor">{d.factor}</span>
              <span class="delay-impact">{d.impact}</span>
              <span class="delay-tip">{d.tip}</span>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Timeline by Emirate</h2>
        <p>The federal laws are the same in every emirate: Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024 for Muslims and Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 for non-Muslims, with Abu Dhabi’s own civil family law for eligible non-Muslims there. What differs is the court system, the fees and the e-services. Here is what is fixed in each. For the wider picture start with our overview of <a href="/en/divorce-in-uae/">divorce in UAE</a>, and for Dubai see how to <a href="/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-dubai/">file for divorce in Dubai</a>.</p>
        <div class="emirate-grid">
          {emirateDiffs.map(e => (
            <div class="emirate-card">
              <h3>{e.emirate}</h3>
              <div class="emirate-typical">{e.typical}</div>
              <p>{e.note}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Timeline for Non-Muslim Expats</h2>
        <p>Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022, in force since 1 February 2023, gives non-Muslims in the UAE a civil route to divorce. It applies to non-Muslim UAE nationals and non-Muslim foreigners resident in the UAE, unless one spouse holds to the law of their home country (Article 1).</p>
        <div class="expat-box">
          <div class="expat-item">
            <span class="expat-label">No-fault divorce</span>
            <p>Either spouse’s wish to separate is enough, with no need to justify it, show harm or blame the other (Article 7). The request is made on the prescribed form, and the divorce takes place by court decision once the other spouse is duly served (Article 8).</p>
          </div>
          <div class="expat-item">
            <span class="expat-label">Judgment at the first hearing</span>
            <p>Divorce cases under this law are not referred to Family Guidance and go straight to court for a judgment at the first hearing (Article 3). How soon that hearing comes depends on registration and on serving the other spouse.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="expat-item">
            <span class="expat-label">Money and children</span>
            <p>Alimony is decided on application after the divorce judgment, at the judge’s discretion, using factors such as the length of the marriage, the wife’s age and each spouse’s finances (Article 9). Custody is a joint right of both parents (Article 10), and either parent can ask the court to decide a joint-custody dispute (Article 10(3)). These questions take as long as their evidence needs.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="expat-item">
            <span class="expat-label">Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court</span>
            <p>Under Abu Dhabi Law No. 14 of 2021 the divorce hearing is set at least 30 days after the application is approved, and the judge issues the divorce at that hearing (Article 14 of the 2022 Regulation). Proceedings run in English, and the ADJD says the court aims to finalise a case within one month of registration. See <a href="/en/how-to-file-for-divorce-in-abu-dhabi/">how to file for divorce in Abu Dhabi</a>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="table-caveat">Note: the DIFC Courts do not have jurisdiction over divorce, custody or other family matters. A valid UAE divorce decree comes from the onshore family courts applying Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022, or from the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court, not from the DIFC.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>How Divorce Timeline Affects Your Visa</h2>
        <p>If your residence is sponsored by your husband, the UAE Government portal says a divorced woman who was living in the UAE on her husband’s visa gets a <strong>one-year extension</strong> of her residence visa from the date of the divorce. It is renewable one time only, needs no substitute sponsor, and can include children who were on their father’s visa. Separately, ICP lists a 180-day grace period after a residence permit is cancelled or expires for widows or divorced women of residents. Confirm which applies to you with ICP or GDRFA before you rely on it.</p>
        <p>Because the divorce date starts these clocks, and the judgment date in a contested case is hard to predict, prepare your next residence option before the final hearing. Read our full guide to the <a href="/en/visa-after-divorce-uae/">UAE visa after divorce</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>What Changed in 2025: Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024</h2>
        <p>The new Personal Status Law took effect on 15 April 2025. Article 1(4) also applies it to divorce proceedings that had no final judgment by then, so a case filed earlier and still pending is decided under the new rules. These are the changes that touch timing.</p>
        <div class="law-table">
          <div class="law-table-header">
            <span>Rule</span>
            <span>What the 2024 Law Says</span>
            <span>Article</span>
            <span>What It Means for Timing</span>
          </div>
          <div class="law-row">
            <span class="law-rule">Family Guidance referral</span>
            <span>The judge may refer if a settlement looks feasible; urgent and temporary matters and cases to prove a divorce are exempt</span>
            <span>Article 8</span>
            <span class="law-positive">No fixed Family Guidance period; urgent orders go straight to the judge</span>
          </div>
          <div class="law-row">
            <span class="law-rule">Arbitrators in harm or discord cases</span>
            <span>Up to 60 days from appointment (the old law allowed 90)</span>
            <span>Article 72</span>
            <span class="law-positive">A shorter cap on this stage</span>
          </div>
          <div class="law-row">
            <span class="law-rule">Khula when the husband refuses</span>
            <span>The court orders khula for an appropriate consideration if he stubbornly refuses</span>
            <span>Article 66</span>
            <span class="law-positive">His refusal does not block the case</span>
          </div>
          <div class="law-row">
            <span class="law-rule">Documenting a talaq</span>
            <span>Within 15 days; late documentation without an acceptable excuse entitles the wife to compensation equal to alimony</span>
            <span>Article 58</span>
            <span class="law-neutral">A clear deadline after a talaq</span>
          </div>
          <div class="law-row">
            <span class="law-rule">Past maintenance</span>
            <span>Claims are not heard for more than two years before filing</span>
            <span>Article 99(3)</span>
            <span class="law-neutral">Claim maintenance without waiting</span>
          </div>
          <div class="law-row">
            <span class="law-rule">Custody age</span>
            <span>Custody ends at 18; at 15 the child may choose which parent to live with</span>
            <span>Articles 122 and 123</span>
            <span class="law-neutral">Sets how long custody arrangements run</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="law-note">Non-Muslim expat divorces remain governed by Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 (in force since 1 February 2023), which is separate from the 2024 reform.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Step by Step: Preparing and Filing an Agreed Divorce</h2>
        <p>For an agreed divorce, these are the steps in order. The law fixes only some of the time limits along the way; the rest depends on your court, your documents and how quickly the other spouse is served. A contested case follows the same order but adds evidence, experts and often appeals.</p>
        <div class="week-timeline">

          <div class="week-block">
            <div class="week-badge">Step 1</div>
            <div class="week-content">
              <h3>Initial Lawyer Consultation and Case Assessment</h3>
              <p>Book a consultation with a UAE-licensed family lawyer; only licensed lawyers may give legal advice and represent you before the courts (Article 6 of Federal Decree-Law No. 34 of 2022). The lawyer reviews your marriage certificate, identifies the applicable law (Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, the 2022 civil law for non-Muslims, or Abu Dhabi Law No. 14 of 2021), and explains which time limits apply to your route. You will discuss whether you both agree and whether children or shared assets are involved, and leave with a document checklist.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="week-block">
            <div class="week-badge">Step 2</div>
            <div class="week-content">
              <h3>Document Gathering</h3>
              <p>Collect the core documents: marriage certificate, passports, Emirates IDs and residence details for both spouses, and children’s birth certificates if applicable. A marriage certificate issued abroad must be legalised before a UAE court will use it: Dubai Courts ask for foreign documents legalised by the issuing country’s foreign ministry, then the UAE embassy in that country, then the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs. The UAE is not a party to the Apostille Convention, so an apostille alone is not enough. Start early, because how long this takes depends on the issuing country.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="week-block">
            <div class="week-badge">Step 3</div>
            <div class="week-content">
              <h3>Legalisation and Arabic Translation</h3>
              <p>Dubai Courts ask for documents with a legal Arabic translation. Legalisation of the marriage certificate follows the chain for your home country: for UK nationals the first step is legalisation by the Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office; for Indian nationals it is attestation by the Ministry of External Affairs. Either way, UAE embassy legalisation and UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs attestation follow. Get translation quotes in advance, because the cost depends on the number of pages.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="week-block">
            <div class="week-badge">Step 4</div>
            <div class="week-content">
              <h3>Family Guidance and Filing</h3>
              <p>In Dubai, a Muslim couple starts with the Dubai Courts Family Conciliation service, which has no fee and ends in a settlement, a waiver, file closure or a referral to court. The personal status claim is then filed on the Dubai Courts website or at an Al Adheed centre with the referral attached, at AED 100 per application capped at AED 2,000 (Dubai Law No. 21 of 2015). Elsewhere, the supervising judge may refer you to Family Guidance (Article 8 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024). The law sets no time limit for this stage, and a settlement approved by the judge has the force of an execution writ. Pay the court fee within 3 business days of the day after the payment notice, or the registration is void (Article 47 of Federal Decree-Law No. 42 of 2022).</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="week-block">
            <div class="week-badge">Step 5</div>
            <div class="week-content">
              <h3>First Court Hearing</h3>
              <p>The court serves the other spouse, who has 10 business days from service to file a defence (Article 48 of Federal Decree-Law No. 42 of 2022). Both spouses, or their representatives, appear before the judge. If every term is agreed, the court can rule on that basis. If any point is disputed, even one financial term, the case moves onto a contested track with evidence and possibly an expert, so resolve all terms before this hearing if you can.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="week-block">
            <div class="week-badge">Step 6</div>
            <div class="week-content">
              <h3>Judgment and Divorce Documents</h3>
              <p>Once the judgment is issued, each side has 30 days to appeal, counted from the day after the judgment if it was issued in their presence and otherwise from the day after notice (Articles 9 and 10 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024). When that period passes without an appeal the judgment is final, and for a Muslim divorce the iddah runs from that date (Article 83). Ask for several certified copies: you will need them for residence, home-country records and any future remarriage.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>What to Prepare at Each Stage: Document Checklist</h2>
        <p>A missing document at the wrong moment holds up the stage you are in. This checklist maps each item to the stage at which it is needed. Have everything for stages 1 to 3 ready before you file.</p>

        <div class="checklist-stages">

          <div class="checklist-stage">
            <div class="checklist-stage-header">
              <div class="checklist-stage-num">1</div>
              <div>
                <h3>Filing and Family Guidance</h3>
                <span class="checklist-stage-note">Have these ready on day one</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <ul class="checklist-list">
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Original marriage certificate (legalised by the UAE embassy and attested by the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs if issued abroad; an apostille alone is not enough)</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Legal Arabic translation of the marriage certificate</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Valid passports for both parties (copies and originals)</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> UAE residence details for both parties (copies)</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Emirates ID cards for both parties (copies)</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Children’s birth certificates if custody is included (legalised and translated)</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Court fee (in Dubai AED 100 per application, capped at AED 2,000; confirm the fee in other emirates when you register)</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Signed engagement letter with your lawyer and power of attorney if filing through a representative</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="checklist-stage">
            <div class="checklist-stage-header">
              <div class="checklist-stage-num">2</div>
              <div>
                <h3>First Court Hearing</h3>
                <span class="checklist-stage-note">For uncontested cases: have a signed agreement ready</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <ul class="checklist-list">
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Signed settlement or separation agreement covering all terms (if uncontested)</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Arabic translation of the agreement</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Family Guidance referral, if the case went through Family Guidance (Dubai Courts ask for the Family Conciliation referral with a personal status claim)</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Proof of current UAE address for both parties (utility bill or tenancy contract)</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Salary certificate or employment contract, if maintenance is claimed (the court looks at the payer’s means, Article 96)</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> List of jointly owned assets and liabilities</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="checklist-stage">
            <div class="checklist-stage-header">
              <div class="checklist-stage-num">3</div>
              <div>
                <h3>Evidence Exchange (Contested Cases)</h3>
                <span class="checklist-stage-note">Financial and property documents: gather well in advance</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <ul class="checklist-list">
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Recent bank statements for all accounts (for a temporary alimony request after a civil divorce the court looks at the last 6 months, Article 10 of Cabinet Resolution No. 122 of 2023)</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Property ownership documents (title deeds, Dubai Land Department registration)</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Vehicle registration certificates</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Business ownership documents, share certificates, or trade licence if applicable</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Employment contracts and recent payslips (both parties)</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Pension and investment account statements</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Marriage contract showing the agreed mahr</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Any prenuptial or postnuptial agreement (legalised and translated)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="checklist-stage">
            <div class="checklist-stage-header">
              <div class="checklist-stage-num">4</div>
              <div>
                <h3>Hearings and Pleadings (Contested Cases)</h3>
                <span class="checklist-stage-note">Ongoing: submit what the court requests</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <ul class="checklist-list">
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Witness statements (if calling character or financial witnesses)</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Expert financial valuation report (if ordered or requested)</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Social or psychological report on the children (if ordered by the court)</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> School records and medical records for children (custody cases)</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Updated bank statements if your financial position has changed since filing</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Evidence of any domestic abuse or other misconduct (if pleaded)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="checklist-stage">
            <div class="checklist-stage-header">
              <div class="checklist-stage-num">5</div>
              <div>
                <h3>Judgment Stage</h3>
                <span class="checklist-stage-note">Nothing to submit, but prepare your next steps now</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <ul class="checklist-list">
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Decide within the 30-day window whether to appeal (Article 9)</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Court fee for certified copies of the judgment, if charged</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Your residence plan: check with ICP or GDRFA whether the one-year extension for divorced women or a grace period applies to you</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="checklist-stage">
            <div class="checklist-stage-header">
              <div class="checklist-stage-num">6</div>
              <div>
                <h3>Divorce Documents</h3>
                <span class="checklist-stage-note">Request several certified copies at this stage</span>
              </div>
            </div>
            <ul class="checklist-list">
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Several certified copies of the final judgment or divorce certificate</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> A certified English translation for use outside the UAE</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Attestation by the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs for home-country use (the UAE does not issue apostilles), then embassy legalisation if your country requires it</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Check the names, dates and personal details on every copy before you leave the court</li>
              <li><span class="check-icon">&#10003;</span> Store the originals securely</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>After the Decree: Your 30-Day Action Plan</h2>
        <p>The final judgment is not the end of the paperwork. Several practical steps follow, and some of them, such as your residence, run on clocks that start with the divorce. Work through the list below in the first 30 days.</p>

        <div class="action-plan-grid">

          <div class="action-item action-urgent">
            <div class="action-item-header">
              <span class="action-urgency urgent">Days 1 to 7: Urgent</span>
              <h3>Residence</h3>
            </div>
            <p>If you were on your husband’s visa, the UAE Government portal says a divorced woman gets a one-year extension from the date of the divorce, renewable one time only and with no substitute sponsor; you apply with the divorce certificate, proof of housing and proof that you can support yourself. Confirm the rule for your case with ICP or GDRFA, and start on your longer-term residence early. See our full guide: <a href="/en/visa-after-divorce-uae/">Visa After Divorce UAE</a>.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="action-item action-urgent">
            <div class="action-item-header">
              <span class="action-urgency urgent">Days 1 to 14: Urgent</span>
              <h3>HR and Employment Records</h3>
            </div>
            <p>Tell your HR or PRO department, because a change in your residence status affects your employment file. If your employer sponsors your visa, confirm the divorce does not change your employment terms. If you were on a spouse visa and now need employer sponsorship, start the request straight away. Some employers ask for the divorce document to update your family file and housing allowance category.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="action-item">
            <div class="action-item-header">
              <span class="action-urgency standard">Days 1 to 14</span>
              <h3>Children’s School Notification</h3>
            </div>
            <p>Tell your children’s school or nursery about the change in family status. Schools need to update authorised pickup lists, emergency contact records and fee-payer details. Give the school a certified copy of the custody order so it knows who decides what.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="action-item">
            <div class="action-item-header">
              <span class="action-urgency standard">Days 7 to 21</span>
              <h3>Bank Accounts and Joint Property</h3>
            </div>
            <p>Close or restructure joint bank accounts after the decree, remove the ex-spouse as a signatory from any account you keep, and cancel supplementary cards issued in the ex-spouse’s name on your account. For jointly owned property, ask your lawyer and the land department what they need to register a transfer or sale under the judgment.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="action-item">
            <div class="action-item-header">
              <span class="action-urgency standard">Days 7 to 21</span>
              <h3>Mahr: Return or Claim</h3>
            </div>
            <p>Check that the judgment deals with the mahr. Any deferred mahr falls due on irrevocable separation (Article 46), and a wife divorced before intercourse or valid seclusion is entitled to half the named mahr (Article 47(3)). In a khula where the dowry is the consideration, returning what she received is enough and the rest is dropped (Article 66). If the judgment is silent on mahr, take advice. See our guide to <a href="/en/mahr-divorce-uae/">mahr in a UAE divorce</a>.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="action-item">
            <div class="action-item-header">
              <span class="action-urgency standard">Days 14 to 30</span>
              <h3>Home Country Records</h3>
            </div>
            <p>Check whether your home country needs the UAE divorce recorded or recognised before you remarry there or update your civil status. Authorities abroad usually ask for the UAE document attested by the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs and legalised by their embassy. See our guide to <a href="/en/divorce-certificate-attestation-uae/">divorce certificate attestation</a>, and if you are British, whether a <a href="/en/uae-divorce-recognised-uk/">UAE divorce is recognised in the UK</a>.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="action-item">
            <div class="action-item-header">
              <span class="action-urgency standard">Days 14 to 30</span>
              <h3>Insurance, Wills, and Beneficiaries</h3>
            </div>
            <p>Review your life insurance beneficiary designations and your employer’s group life and medical cover, and check whether your ex-spouse is still listed. If you have a will, such as a DIFC or ADGM will, review it or redraft it.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="action-item">
            <div class="action-item-header">
              <span class="action-urgency standard">Days 21 to 30</span>
              <h3>Name Change (if applicable)</h3>
            </div>
            <p>A return to a pre-marriage surname usually starts with your home country’s passport or civil registry authority. Once your passport changes, update your UAE residence record, Emirates ID, bank accounts, employment records, driving licence and vehicle registration to match. See our guide to <a href="/en/name-change-after-divorce-uae/">changing your name after divorce</a>.</p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Can I Speed Up My UAE Divorce?</h2>
        <p>Partly. The law fixes some time limits and the court sets its own schedule, but preparation and agreement are in your hands, and they decide much of how long the rest takes. Here is what works.</p>

        <div class="speed-tips">

          <div class="speed-tip">
            <div class="speed-tip-header">
              <span class="speed-icon">01</span>
              <h3>Reach Full Agreement Before You File</h3>
            </div>
            <p>This is the single most powerful lever. No law fixes how long a divorce takes, but an agreed divorce is usually far quicker than a contested one, and most of the gap is time spent arguing over money and children, not procedure. If you need money while a contested case runs, ask the court for <a href="/en/temporary-alimony-uae/">temporary alimony</a> (Article 100). Before filing, agree in writing on: division of assets and debts, custody schedule and decision-making authority, child maintenance amount and payment method, spousal alimony (if applicable), and mahr arrangements. A settlement reached in Family Guidance and approved by the judge has the force of an execution writ (Article 8).</p>
          </div>

          <div class="speed-tip">
            <div class="speed-tip-header">
              <span class="speed-icon">02</span>
              <h3>Have Every Document Legalised and Translated Before the First Hearing</h3>
            </div>
            <p>Dubai Courts ask for foreign documents legalised by the issuing country’s foreign ministry, the UAE embassy there and the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs, with a legal Arabic translation. Complete the chain for your marriage certificate, any prenuptial agreement and the children’s birth certificates before you file, so the case does not wait for a missing document.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="speed-tip">
            <div class="speed-tip-header">
              <span class="speed-icon">03</span>
              <h3>Use the 2022 Civil Divorce Law if You Are Non-Muslim</h3>
            </div>
            <p>Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 applies to non-Muslim UAE nationals and non-Muslim foreigners resident in the UAE unless one spouse holds to the law of their home country (Article 1). Under it either spouse can ask for a divorce without justifying it or showing harm (Articles 7 and 8), the case is not referred to Family Guidance, and the divorce is decided at the first hearing (Article 3). Confirm with your lawyer that your case is filed under this law from the outset.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="speed-tip">
            <div class="speed-tip-header">
              <span class="speed-icon">04</span>
              <h3>Non-Muslims: Consider the Abu Dhabi Civil Family Court</h3>
            </div>
            <p>Under Abu Dhabi Law No. 14 of 2021 the divorce hearing is set at least 30 days after the application is approved and the judge issues the divorce at that hearing (Article 14 of the 2022 Regulation). Proceedings run in English, and the ADJD says the court aims to finalise a case within one month of registration. The ADJD also says applicants must be Abu Dhabi residents or have married at the court, and come from a country not governed by Sharia law. One point to clear up early: the DIFC Courts do not hear divorce or other family cases, so a valid UAE divorce cannot come from the DIFC.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="speed-tip">
            <div class="speed-tip-header">
              <span class="speed-icon">05</span>
              <h3>Know When the Judgment Becomes Final</h3>
            </div>
            <p>After judgment, each party has 30 days to appeal (Article 9), counted from the day after the judgment if it was issued in their presence and otherwise from the day after notice (Article 10). Until that window closes, the judgment is not final, and for a Muslim divorce the iddah runs from the date it becomes final (Article 83). Plan your residence and paperwork around that date, not the hearing date.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="speed-tip">
            <div class="speed-tip-header">
              <span class="speed-icon">06</span>
              <h3>Appoint an Experienced Family Lawyer from Day One</h3>
            </div>
            <p>You may appear in person or through a lawyer (Article 52 of Federal Decree-Law No. 42 of 2022), but only a UAE-licensed lawyer may give legal advice and represent you (Article 6 of Federal Decree-Law No. 34 of 2022). A lawyer who regularly practises in your emirate’s family courts knows the forms and filing sequence there. Small procedural errors, such as a missing stamp, the wrong application form or a name spelled differently across documents, are the kind of thing that holds a case up.</p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div class="speed-summary-box">
          <h3>Time Limits the Law Actually Fixes</h3>
          <div class="speed-summary-grid">
            <div class="speed-saving">
              <span class="saving-action">Family Guidance (Article 8)</span>
              <span class="saving-time">No statutory period</span>
            </div>
            <div class="speed-saving">
              <span class="saving-action">Arbitrators in harm cases (Article 72)</span>
              <span class="saving-time">Up to 60 days</span>
            </div>
            <div class="speed-saving">
              <span class="saving-action">Appeal and cassation (Article 9)</span>
              <span class="saving-time">30 days each</span>
            </div>
            <div class="speed-saving">
              <span class="saving-action">Documenting a talaq (Article 58)</span>
              <span class="saving-time">Within 15 days</span>
            </div>
            <div class="speed-saving">
              <span class="saving-action">Non-Muslim civil divorce (2022 law, Article 3)</span>
              <span class="saving-time">First hearing</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <FAQSection items={faqItems} />
      </section>

      <div class="cta-box">
        <h2>Concerned About How Long Your Case Will Take?</h2>
        <p>A UAE-licensed family lawyer can tell you which time limits apply to your case after reviewing the facts, and identify any steps you can take now to avoid delays.</p>
        <LeadForm source="divorce-timeline-uae" />
      </div>

